.TH XD\-DISKBENCH 1 2026-10-27 XeonDesktop "XeonDesktop Manual"
.SH NAME
xd\-diskbench \- Disk Benchmark and Health
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY xd\-diskbench
.OP \-\-page n
.OP \-\-drive mount-point
.OP \-\-folder directory
.OP \-\-disk device
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xd\-diskbench
measures how fast a drive reads and writes, and shows each drive's
S.M.A.R.T. health.
It has no Windows original; it sits in Administrative Tools, and a
drive's Properties > Tools in
.BR xd\-files (1)
opens it with Test Now..., as Disk Management's Health... does for a
disk.
.SS Benchmark
Drive lists the mounted file systems that can be written to, with their
free space; Folder... picks any other folder.
The test file goes in a hidden folder,
.IR .xd\-diskbench ,
at the root of the file system, or in your home folder on it when the
root can't be written; a folder's test file goes in the same hidden folder
inside it.
The test file has a new name each time and is deleted when the test
ends, whether it finished, was stopped or failed; a test file left by a
crash is deleted the next time a test runs there.
Before the test, the free space is checked (the file and 64 MiB to spare),
and the file is preallocated and filled.
.PP
Runs (1 to 9) is how often each test runs; the best run is shown.
Test size is the size of the test file, from 16 MiB to 64 GiB.
Units shows the results in MB/s or GB/s (1 MB = 1,000,000 bytes),
operations a second (IOPS), or the average time an operation takes (μs);
every cell's tooltip has all three.
.PP
The default tests are:
.IP 1. 4
Sequential, 1 MiB blocks, queue depth 8, 1 thread
.IP 2. 4
Sequential, 1 MiB blocks, queue depth 1, 1 thread
.IP 3. 4
Random, 4 KiB blocks, queue depth 32, 1 thread
.IP 4. 4
Random, 4 KiB blocks, queue depth 1, 1 thread
.PP
Each test's button runs its read and write test; All runs every test's
read, then every write.
While a test runs its button reads Stop.
Settings... changes each test's block size, queue depth and thread count,
how long each run measures (5 seconds by default) and the wait between
tests (5 seconds), and whether the test data is random (the default) or
zeros.
Its profiles are Default; Peak performance, with deeper queues and more
threads; and Realistic, with shallow queues and a fifth test of random
4 KiB blocks, 70% reads and 30% writes, whose reads and writes are shown
in the two columns.
Reset puts everything back.
.PP
The I/O goes through io_uring, a ring for each thread, with the file
opened with O_DIRECT so the page cache doesn't make the results look
better than the drive is.
A sequential test's threads each read or write their own part of the
file; a random test's go anywhere in it, aligned to the block size.
On a file system that doesn't allow O_DIRECT (tmpfs, some FUSE file
systems) the test uses buffered I/O, with fdatasync after writes, and
the results are marked (cached).
.PP
Edit > Copy (Ctrl+C) puts the results on the clipboard as text, with the
drive, its file system, the test size, the settings and the date.
.SS Health
The drive list shows each drive with its health's colour.
UDisks2 provides the data: Drive.Ata for ATA drives,
NVMe.Controller (UDisks2 2.10 or later) for NVMe drives.
.PP
Health status is one of:
.TP
.B Bad
the drive says it is failing (SmartFailing), an attribute's current value
is at or under its threshold (a threshold of 0 never fails), or an NVMe
drive reports a critical warning.
.TP
.B Caution
the drive has reallocated (attribute 05), pending (C5) or uncorrectable
(C6) sectors; an NVMe drive's available spare is under its threshold, or
90% or more of its rated life is used.
.TP
.B Good
none of these.
.TP
.B Unknown
the drive has no S.M.A.R.T. data: USB drives, memory cards and virtual
disks usually don't, or it's turned off.
.PP
Details shows the model, firmware, serial number, capacity, interface,
rotation rate, the features UDisks2 reports (S.M.A.R.T., APM, AAM, write
cache; it doesn't report TRIM or NCQ), power-on hours and count, and the
data read and written where the drive counts them.
An ATA drive's attributes are listed with their raw values as UDisks2
reads them (sectors, hours, °C); rows at or under their threshold are
red, sector rows above zero yellow.
An NVMe drive's health log is listed instead.
.PP
View > Refresh (F5) asks UDisks2 to read the drive's data again, which
polkit may ask an administrator's permission for.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-page " n"
Open on tab
.IR n :
0 (Benchmark) or 1 (Health).
.TP
.BI \-\-drive " mount-point"
Select the drive mounted there, on both tabs.
.TP
.BI \-\-folder " directory"
Test in
.I directory
(added to the Drive list).
.TP
.BI \-\-disk " device"
Select the disk on the Health tab by its block device
.RI ( /dev/sda ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/xeondesktop/diskbench.ini
The settings, in
.BR [Benchmark] .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xd\-admintools (1),
.BR xd\-diskmgmt (1),
.BR xd\-files (1),
.BR udisksctl (1)
